    "text": "To solve the equation \\((z^2 - 3z + 1)^2 - 3(z^2 - 3z + 1) + 1 = z\\), we start by making a substitution to simplify the equation. Let \\( w = z^2 - 3z + 1 \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\n\\[ w^2 - 3w + 1 = z. \\]\n\nNext, we need to express \\( z^2 - 3z + 1 \\) in terms of \\( w \\). Notice that \\( w = z^2 - 3z + 1 \\), so we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[ w^2 - 3w + 1 = z. \\]\n\nWe need to find the values of \\( w \\) such that the above equation holds. To do this, we substitute \\( z = w \\) into the expression \\( z^2 - 3z + 1 \\):\n\n\\[ z^2 - 3z + 1 = w^2 - 3w + 1. \\]\n\nThis equation is always true, so we need to solve the quadratic equation in \\( w \\):\n\n\\[ w^2 - 3w + 1 = z. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the values of \\( w \\) that satisfy this equation. We substitute back \\( z = w \\) and solve for \\( w \\):\n\n\\[ w^2 - 3w + 1 = w. \\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[ w^2 - 4w + 1 = 0. \\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( w = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -4 \\), and \\( c = 1 \\):\n\n\\[ w = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{12}}{2} = \\frac{4 \\pm 2\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 2 \\pm \\sqrt{3}. \\]\n\nSo, the solutions for \\( w \\) are \\( w = 2 + \\sqrt{3} \\) and \\( w = 2 - \\sqrt{3} \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the corresponding values of \\( z \\). For \\( w = 2 + \\sqrt{3} \\):\n\n\\[ z^2 - 3z + 1 = 2 + \\sqrt{3}. \\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[ z^2 - 3z - 1 + \\sqrt{3} = 0. \\]\n\nUsing the quadratic formula \\( z = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\) with \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -3 \\), and \\( c = -1 + \\sqrt{3} \\):\n\n\\[ z = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 + 4(1 - \\sqrt{3})}}{2} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{13 - 4\\sqrt{3}}}{2}. \\]\n\nFor \\( w = 2 - \\sqrt{3} \\):\n\n\\[ z^2 - 3z + 1 = 2 - \\sqrt{3}. \\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[ z^2 - 3z - 1 - \\sqrt{3} = 0. \\]\n\nUsing the quadratic formula \\( z = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\) with \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -3 \\), and \\( c = -1 - \\sqrt{3} \\):\n\n\\[ z = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 + 4(1 + \\sqrt{3})}}{2} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{13 + 4\\sqrt{3}}}{2}. \\]\n\nThus, the solutions to the equation are:\n\n\\[ z = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{13 - 4\\sqrt{3}}}{2}, \\quad z = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{13 - 4\\sqrt{3}}}{2}, \\quad z = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{13 + 4\\sqrt{3}}}{2}, \\quad z = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{13 + 4\\sqrt{3}}}{2}. \\]\n\nTherefore, the solutions are:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{3 + \\sqrt{13 - 4\\sqrt{3}}}{2}, \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{13 - 4\\sqrt{3}}}{2}, \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{13 + 4\\sqrt{3}}}{2}, \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{13 + 4\\sqrt{3}}}{2}}. \\]",
